\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3}{\ignorespaces The one-shot selection loss $\mathrm  {frag}(\sigma _e)$ of a landscape (Equation~\ref {eq:frag}, computed on 256 quasi-random designs with no optimizer) against the trajectory cost the sweep measured on it, one point per landscape and magnitude, error from the first rating. Shape and shade both encode the magnitude. Nine of the eighty cells cannot be placed on log axes, eight because $\mathrm  {frag}$ is exactly zero and one because the measured cost is negative; both correlations in the panel are over all eighty. The pooled value largely restates that both quantities grow with $\sigma _e$, which is why the within-magnitude range is given beside it.}}{7}{figure.3}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:frag}{{3}{7}{The one-shot selection loss $\frag (\sigma _e)$ of a landscape (Equation~\ref {eq:frag}, computed on 256 quasi-random designs with no optimizer) against the trajectory cost the sweep measured on it, one point per landscape and magnitude, error from the first rating. Shape and shade both encode the magnitude. Nine of the eighty cells cannot be placed on log axes, eight because $\frag $ is exactly zero and one because the measured cost is negative; both correlations in the panel are over all eighty. The pooled value largely restates that both quantities grow with $\sigma _e$, which is why the within-magnitude range is given beside it}{figure.3}{}}
