\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {25}{\ignorespaces Budget-neutral arms: share of the standard process's cost of error recovered on the deployed design, with 95\% landscape-bootstrap intervals. Negative means the change does worse under error than the standard process. Each arm is scored on the magnitudes and onsets it was run at, which differ between arms, so a row is a share of its own reference's cost and the column is not a ranking; the gross-fault rows are $0.25\sigma $ from the first rating only. The lost-rating arm divides by a reference cost of $0.010$, near zero and negative on nine of twenty landscapes, so it is given as an absolute loss in points of achievable improvement rather than a share. Above the rule, changes to the search; below it, changes to the model of how the rating was produced.}}{28}{table.25}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:budgetneutral}{{25}{28}{Budget-neutral arms: share of the standard process's cost of error recovered on the deployed design, with 95\% landscape-bootstrap intervals. Negative means the change does worse under error than the standard process. Each arm is scored on the magnitudes and onsets it was run at, which differ between arms, so a row is a share of its own reference's cost and the column is not a ranking; the gross-fault rows are $0.25\sigma $ from the first rating only. The lost-rating arm divides by a reference cost of $0.010$, near zero and negative on nine of twenty landscapes, so it is given as an absolute loss in points of achievable improvement rather than a share. Above the rule, changes to the search; below it, changes to the model of how the rating was produced}{table.25}{}}
